Two distinct things in this tree derive from google/brotli
(https://github.com/google/brotli), which is distributed under the MIT licence
reproduced verbatim at the end of this file.

1. The static tables in `omni-core/src/brotli_tables.rs` and the raw dictionary
   in `omni-core/src/brotli_dict.bin`. These are the 122 784-byte static
   dictionary, its `size_bits`/`offsets` index, the 121 word transforms and
   their prefix/suffix pool, and the UTF-8 and signed context lookup tables —
   copied verbatim from `c/common/{dictionary,transform,context}.c`. RFC 7932 is
   *defined* by these tables; there is no independent version of them to
   reconstruct from, so they are transcribed rather than reinvented, and a
   transcription error in them can only be caught by decoding real streams,
   which is what item 2 is for.

2. The `.br` streams in this directory. `tools/brotli-fixture.py` compresses a
   fixed corpus with `libbrotli` at quality levels 1, 5 and 11; each `<name>.br`
   is that library's output and each `<name>.raw` is the original input, listed
   in `manifest.txt`. The vectors are libbrotli's own bytes; nothing here was
   hand-edited. `omni-core`'s decoder must reproduce every `.raw` from its `.br`
   byte for byte — that differential check against the one reference
   implementation is the only oracle brotli has, since it is a single library
   rather than a spec with a field of independent implementations. Re-run the
   script to regenerate them; the corpus and quality levels are defined there.

